+    <div class="docs-meta"><span>Receiver trust and maritime</span><a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/CONNECT.md">Edit on GitHub</a><span>Source: <code>docs/CONNECT.md</code></span></div>
+    <div class="prose"><h1 id="kshana-connect-mapping-files">Kshana Connect: mapping files</h1>
+<p>A mapping file (<code>kshana.connect/1</code>, TOML) tells Kshana how to read an export it has no reader
+for, and turns it into a <a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/OBS-SCHEMA.md"><code>kshana.obs/1</code></a> observation file. No code is written:
+the file names the columns, their units and the time format.</p>
+<p>Step by step, for a monitoring station: <a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/guides/MONITORING-STATION.md">guides/MONITORING-STATION.md</a>.
+Step by step, for scoring an interference classifier on the benchmark: <a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/guides/BENCHMARK-CLASSES.md">guides/BENCHMARK-CLASSES.md</a>.</p>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">kshana connect map export.csv --mapping export.connect.toml --station station.toml --out site.obs.jsonl
+kshana obs check site.obs.jsonl</code></pre>
+<p>Three worked examples are in <a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/examples/connect"><code>examples/connect/</code></a>: an Android raw
+measurement CSV (one row per satellite, satellites from a constellation code and a number), a
+timing receiver&#39;s semicolon-separated monitoring export (one column per satellite, GPS week and
+time of week, height in feet) and a monitoring service&#39;s nested JSON (position in radians).
+<code>tests/connect_examples.rs</code> maps all three.</p>
+<h2 id="the-file">The file<a class="anchor" href="#the-file" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<pre data-lang="toml"><code class="hljs language-ini"><span class="hljs-attr">schema</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;kshana.connect/1&quot;</span>
+
+<span class="hljs-section">[source]</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">format</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;csv&quot;</span>          <span class="hljs-comment"># csv, tsv, json or jsonl</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">delimiter</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;;&quot;</span>         <span class="hljs-comment"># csv only; one character, default &quot;,&quot;</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">skip_lines</span> = <span class="hljs-number">0</span>          <span class="hljs-comment"># lines before the header (csv, tsv)</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">comment</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;#&quot;</span>           <span class="hljs-comment"># lines starting with this are ignored (csv, tsv)</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">records</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;/data/epochs&quot;</span> <span class="hljs-comment"># json only: JSON pointer to the array of records</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">filter</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;type&quot;</span>, equals = [<span class="hljs-string">&quot;Raw&quot;</span>] }  <span class="hljs-comment"># keep only these rows</span>
+
+<span class="hljs-section">[time]</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">column</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;tow&quot;</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">format</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;gps-week-tow&quot;</span> <span class="hljs-comment"># rfc3339, unix-s, unix-ms, gps-s, gps-week-tow or pattern</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">week_column</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;week&quot;</span>    <span class="hljs-comment"># gps-week-tow: the full GPS week</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">pattern</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;%d/%m/%Y %H:%M:%S&quot;</span>  <span class="hljs-comment"># pattern: %Y %m %d %H %M %S (seconds may have a fraction)</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">scale</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;utc&quot;</span>           <span class="hljs-comment"># rfc3339, unix-* and pattern: utc (default), gps or tai</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">group_tolerance_s</span> = <span class="hljs-number">0.0005</span>  <span class="hljs-comment"># rows this close in time are one epoch</span>
+
+<span class="hljs-section">[fix]</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">lat</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;lat&quot;</span>, unit = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;deg&quot;</span> }   <span class="hljs-comment"># deg or rad</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">lon</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;lon&quot;</span>, unit = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;deg&quot;</span> }
+<span class="hljs-attr">height</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;alt&quot;</span>, unit = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;ft&quot;</span> } <span class="hljs-comment"># m, ft or km</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">n_used</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;nsat&quot;</span> }
+
+<span class="hljs-section">[signals]</span>
+<span class="hljs-comment"># One row per satellite ...</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">sat</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;prn&quot;</span> }  <span class="hljs-comment"># G05, G5 or &quot;G 5&quot;; or number_column with system / system_column + system_map</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">cn0</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;cn0&quot;</span>, unit = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;dB-Hz&quot;</span> }
+<span class="hljs-attr">band_column</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;signal&quot;</span>    <span class="hljs-comment"># or band = &quot;L1&quot; (the default)</span>
+<span class="hljs-comment"># ... or one column per satellite:</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">wide</span> = { G05 = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;cn0_g05&quot;</span>, E11 = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;cn0_e11&quot;</span> }
+
+<span class="hljs-section">[receiver]</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">agc</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;agc&quot;</span> }          <span class="hljs-comment"># native units</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">jam_ind</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;jamming_indicator&quot;</span> }
+<span class="hljs-attr">spoofing</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;spoof&quot;</span> }   <span class="hljs-comment"># receiver&#x27;s own flag: 0 unknown, 1 none, 2 indicated, 3 affirmed</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">jamming</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;jam&quot;</span> }
+<span class="hljs-attr">pdop</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;pdop&quot;</span> }
+<span class="hljs-attr">hdop</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;hdop&quot;</span> }
+<span class="hljs-attr">vdop</span> = { column = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;vdop&quot;</span> }</code></pre>
+<p>Every field takes <code>scale</code> and <code>offset</code> (applied after the unit: <code>value × scale + offset</code>), and
+in JSON a <code>column</code> starting with <code>/</code> is a JSON pointer into nested objects.</p>
+<h2 id="rules">Rules<a class="anchor" href="#rules" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<ul>
+<li>Rows with the same time (within <code>group_tolerance_s</code>) are one epoch, so long and wide exports
+both work, and position rows interleaved with satellite rows join their epoch.</li>
+<li>An empty cell (or a JSON <code>null</code>) is a missing value; anything else that is not a number is an
+error naming the line and the column. Nothing is guessed.</li>
+<li>Times must not go backwards; a row that does is an error naming it, never a silent reorder.</li>
+<li>GPS and TAI times are converted to UTC with the leap-second table; the observation file&#39;s
+<code>time_label</code> is UTC.</li>
+<li>A CSV header without a column the mapping names is a mapping error.</li>
+<li>The output header&#39;s <code>source_format</code> is <code>connect:&lt;format&gt;</code>.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2 id="conformance-vectors">Conformance vectors<a class="anchor" href="#conformance-vectors" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>conformance/connect/</code> holds one folder per adapter with a <code>vector.toml</code> (input, mapping file or
+built-in reader, station, expected output). <code>kshana connect check conformance/connect</code> runs them
+all and exits 1 on any byte difference, naming the first differing line; <code>--bless</code> records the
+expected outputs after a reviewed change. CI runs the folder (<code>tests/connect_conformance.rs</code>).
+Every new adapter comes with its vector.</p>
+<p>Not in this version: Parquet and HDF5 inputs (they need large dependencies; a conversion to CSV
+or JSON Lines is the route for now) and live inputs (A3a).</p>
+<h2 id="live-watching-a-directory-library">Live: watching a directory (library)<a class="anchor" href="#live-watching-a-directory-library" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana::connect_watch::step</code> follows the files a logger writes or rotates into a directory and returns
+the <code>kshana.obs/1</code> records that became complete since the last step, through the same mapping file.</p>
+<ul>
+<li>The files whose names end with the chosen suffix are read in name order, as one continuous log: a
+later file&#39;s comment lines and header row are dropped. So an epoch whose rows a rotation split is
+still one epoch, and every <code>t_s</code> counts from the directory&#39;s first epoch.</li>
+<li>Only complete lines are read; a line still being written waits for the next step.</li>
+<li>The last epoch is held back until rows after it arrive, because more rows of it may still be written.
+<code>flush</code> releases it at the end of a run.</li>
+<li>Files must be in time order by name. A single JSON document is refused (use <code>jsonl</code>).</li>
+<li>It polls, with no operating-system file events, so it behaves the same on every platform and on
+network file systems. The directory is re-read on each step, so move processed files away from time
+to time.</li>
+<li>Checked by replaying a log written in pieces and rotated part-way through an epoch: the result is the
+same as <code>kshana connect map</code> on the whole log (<code>tests/connect_watch.rs</code>).</li>
+<li>From the command line: <code>kshana connect watch &lt;dir&gt; --mapping &lt;connect.toml&gt; --station &lt;station.toml&gt;</code>
+prints new records as JSON lines every <code>--interval</code> seconds (default 2). <code>--suffix</code> picks the files
+(default: the mapping&#39;s format, for example <code>.csv</code>). <code>--once</code> reads what is there, releases the last
+epoch and exits.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2 id="live-mqtt-subscription-kshanaconnect_mqtt">Live MQTT subscription (<code>kshana::connect_mqtt</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#live-mqtt-subscription-kshanaconnect_mqtt" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana::connect_mqtt</code> subscribes to a topic on an MQTT broker and maps the rows that arrive, with the
+same mapping file, into <code>kshana.obs/1</code> records.</p>
+<ul>
+<li>MQTT 3.1.1 over TCP: clean session, optional user name and password, one topic filter (<code>+</code> and <code>#</code>
+work), QoS 0 or 1. A QoS 1 message is acknowledged. A ping is sent when the line has been quiet for
+half the keep-alive.</li>
+<li>Each message is one or more rows of the mapping&#39;s format (<code>jsonl</code>, <code>csv</code> or <code>tsv</code>). The messages are
+read in arrival order as one continuous log, as <code>connect watch</code> reads a directory, and the last epoch
+is held back until a later row arrives. Publishers send rows without a header, so a CSV or TSV mapping
+needs the header row given (<code>--header</code>).</li>
+<li>Rows already emitted are dropped as soon as their epoch is emitted, and <code>t_s</code> still counts from the
+first epoch of the run. Each message therefore costs the same however long the subscription has run,
+and memory stays bounded.</li>
+<li>TLS is not built in, and the default build adds no crate for MQTT. For a broker that only accepts TLS,
+run a local TLS relay (for example stunnel, or a local broker bridged to it) and subscribe to that.</li>
+<li>Checked against a loopback broker that publishes a log one row per message, alternating QoS 0 and 1:
+the result is the same as <code>kshana connect map</code> on the whole log, with the trimming path exercised
+(<code>tests/connect_mqtt.rs</code>).</li>
+<li>From the command line: <code>kshana connect mqtt &lt;host:port&gt; --topic &lt;filter&gt; --mapping &lt;connect.toml&gt; --station &lt;station.toml&gt;</code> prints records as JSON lines as they complete. <code>--user</code> with
+<code>--password-env &lt;VAR&gt;</code> reads the password from an environment variable, never from the command line.
+<code>--max-messages &lt;n&gt;</code> ends the run after n messages and releases the last epoch.</li>
+<li>Many stations on one broker: subscribe with a wildcard (<code>--topic &#39;site/+/raw&#39;</code>) and add <code>--per-topic</code>.
+Each topic then gets its own feed, and its records carry the station id <code>&lt;station.id&gt;/&lt;topic&gt;</code>,
+with one header per station before its first epoch.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2 id="live-nats-input-kshanaconnect_nats">Live NATS input (<code>kshana::connect_nats</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#live-nats-input-kshanaconnect_nats" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana connect nats &lt;host:port&gt; --subject &lt;s&gt; --mapping &lt;connect.toml&gt; --station &lt;station.toml&gt;</code>
+subscribes to a NATS subject and maps the rows that arrive, through the same feeds as MQTT.</p>
+<ul>
+<li>NATS client protocol over TCP: <code>CONNECT</code> (with <code>--user</code> and <code>--password-env &lt;VAR&gt;</code>, or
+<code>--token-env &lt;VAR&gt;</code>; secrets come from environment variables only), <code>SUB</code>, then <code>MSG</code> and <code>HMSG</code>
+(headers are skipped). A server <code>PING</code> is answered with <code>PONG</code>, and a <code>-ERR</code> is reported. A lost
+connection is made again, as for MQTT.</li>
+<li><code>--per-topic</code> gives each subject its own feed (station id <code>&lt;station.id&gt;/&lt;subject&gt;</code>), for a wildcard
+subject such as <code>gnss.*.raw</code>.</li>
+<li>TLS is not built in: for a server that requires it, use a local TLS relay.</li>
+<li>Checked against a loopback server that publishes a log one row per message on two subjects, with a
+header-carrying <code>HMSG</code> and a <code>PING</code> in between. Each subject&#39;s epochs equal <code>kshana connect map</code>
+on that log (<code>tests/connect_nats.rs</code>).</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Kafka is not read yet. A Kafka topic can reach Kshana today through a bridge to MQTT or NATS.</p>
+<h2 id="live-websocket-input-kshanaconnect_ws">Live WebSocket input (<code>kshana::connect_ws</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#live-websocket-input-kshanaconnect_ws" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana::connect_ws</code> reads rows streamed over a WebSocket (RFC 6455) and maps them like an MQTT
+subscription, through the same continuous-log feed.</p>
+<ul>
+<li><code>ws://</code> over TCP. The upgrade key is random and the server&#39;s <code>Sec-WebSocket-Accept</code> is checked. Text
+and binary messages are read, fragments are joined, a ping is answered with a pong, and a close ends
+the stream (the held last epoch is released then). Frames the client sends are masked. Messages over
+16 MiB are refused.</li>
+<li><code>wss://</code> is not built in. Run a local TLS relay and connect to it with <code>ws://</code>.</li>
+<li>Checked against a loopback server that sends one message in two fragments, a ping, one row per
+message and a close: the result is the same as <code>kshana connect map</code> on the whole log; the accept key
+matches the example in RFC 6455 (<code>tests/connect_ws.rs</code>).</li>
+<li>From the command line: <code>kshana connect ws &lt;ws://host:port/path&gt; --mapping &lt;connect.toml&gt; --station &lt;station.toml&gt; [--header &lt;row&gt;] [--max-messages &lt;n&gt;]</code>.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2 id="load-test-kshana-connect-load-test">Load test (<code>kshana connect load-test</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#load-test-kshana-connect-load-test" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana connect load-test</code> runs many synthetic stations through the live feeds: by default 1,000
+stations at 1 Hz for 10 s, each sending one CSV row per epoch in the layout of
+<code>examples/connect/timing-monitor.csv</code>. It passes when the feeds keep up with the offered load, 99 % of
+messages take at most 250 ms, and every epoch comes back.</p>
+<ul>
+<li><code>--via memory</code> (default) puts the rows straight into the feeds as fast as they are taken, and measures
+how many messages one core maps a second.</li>
+<li><code>--via mqtt</code> has a loopback broker publish every station&#39;s rows on its own topic, paced at the real
+rate. One wildcard subscription splits them per topic, and the test measures the time from a message
+being sent to its records being returned.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Measured with a release build on one core of a cloud machine on 6 Nov 2026 (1,000 stations, 1 Hz,
+10 s; your machine will differ, so run it there):</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Via</th>
+<th>Capacity</th>
+<th>Time per message (median / 99th percentile / max)</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>memory</td>
+<td>40,659 messages/s against 1,000 offered</td>
+<td>0.022 / 0.042 / 0.276 ms</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>mqtt</td>
+<td>(as memory)</td>
+<td>12.9 / 28.5 / 29.7 ms</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>Over MQTT, all 1,000 stations send in the same instant each second, so most of that time is a message
+waiting behind the others of its second. <code>tests/connect_load.rs</code> runs a smaller load in both modes on
+every change. It also checks that per-topic feeds give each station exactly the epochs of
+<code>kshana connect map</code> on its own log.</p>
+<h2 id="live-conformance-kit-kshana-connect-check-live">Live conformance kit (<code>kshana connect check-live</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#live-conformance-kit-kshana-connect-check-live" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana connect check-live &lt;vector.toml|dir&gt;</code> replays the conformance vectors over MQTT, WebSocket and
+the directory watch and checks ordering, holding, reconnection and latency. The rules are in
+<a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/conformance/connect/README.md"><code>conformance/connect/README.md</code></a>. On the bundled vectors every path
+passes, with a 99th-percentile latency of a few milliseconds on a laptop.</p>
+<h2 id="grid-timing-ieee-c371182-pmu-streams-library-modelled">Grid timing: IEEE C37.118.2 PMU streams (library, MODELLED)<a class="anchor" href="#grid-timing-ieee-c371182-pmu-streams-library-modelled" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana::c37118</code> reads a capture of synchrophasor frames (a TCP stream or a dump of UDP payloads) and
+reports each PMU&#39;s time health: the sync-error bit, the time-quality and unlocked-time classes in the
+<code>STAT</code> word, and the message time quality and leap-second flags in <code>FRACSEC</code>. <code>sync_losses</code> groups
+consecutive sync-error reports into intervals. Every frame&#39;s CRC is checked. A data frame is read only
+after a configuration frame (CFG-2) has given each PMU&#39;s channel layout. Phasor values are stepped over,
+not decoded.</p>
+<p>It is not yet a <code>kshana obs convert</code> reader, because the <code>kshana.obs/1</code> schema has no field for time
+quality. That needs a schema minor version. It is checked against a stream from an independent encoder
+(<code>tests/c37118.rs</code>); no public PMU capture has been checked yet.</p>
+<p>A binary sensor is first decoded by a device file (<code>kshana.device/1</code>, <a href="https://github.com/ashfordeOU/kshana/blob/main/docs/DEVICES.md">DEVICES.md</a>) into JSON
+Lines, which a mapping file then reads like any other JSON Lines export.</p>
+</div>
